Hmm, but a definition could theoretically contain ";"… not in this subset. Fine.

Also, should the name check for `;` happen at definition time? Not needed.

One more point: compile() at definition time resolves user-defined words to names, and at execution time looks them up in the map — but the local-scope test: `: foo 10 ;`, `: foo foo 1 + ;`. At the time of the second definition, words["foo"] = [10]. Compile the body: token "foo" → tok{name:"foo"}. At execution, executing the new foo: tok{name:"foo"} → look up words["foo"] → the new body [foo, 1, +] → infinite recursion!

So we need to compile at definition time: replace the word with its body (inlining) at definition time. That is, the compiled body should contain the resolved body, not the name.

So tok should hold the resolved body:

```go
type tok struct {
	n    int
	body []tok // resolved user-defined word
}
```

At compile time: if the name is in words, tok{body: words[name]} (copy the slice to avoid aliasing issues — well, aliasing is fine since we don't mutate the body). Built-ins stay as name strings.

```go
type tok struct {
	n    int
	name string // built-in name
	body []tok  // inlined user-defined word
}
```

In compile: if it's a number → tok{n}; otherwise, if it's in words → tok{body: words[name]}; otherwise, tok{name: name}.

Execution: if t.body != nil → execute the body; otherwise if t.name != "" → execWord; otherwise push t.n.

This handles the local-scope test correctly, and also handles "can use different words with the same name": `: foo 5 ;`, `: bar foo ;` → bar's body inlines [5]; `: foo 6 ;` → foo becomes [6]; `bar foo` → 5, 6. ✓

And "can define a word that uses a word with the same name": the second foo inlines the old foo's body [10] → [10, 1, +] → 11 ✓.

